Header guard for HierarchicalNSW::load() — turn a corrupted index header into a loud failure
instead of a malloc on garbage.

PROBLEM. load() reads all 21 header fields with no validation and then sizes two allocations
directly from them:

    centroids_memory_   = malloc(num_cluster_ * padded_dim_ * sizeof(float));
    data_level0_memory_ = malloc(max_elements_ * size_data_per_element_);

followed by reads of those lengths. A flip in any count or size field is therefore not a wrong
answer, it is an allocation on a value that was never written — the process dies inside load(),
before a single query runs, with no indication of which field was wrong.

This is reached far more often than "someone corrupted the header" suggests. The header is only
156 bytes, but the centroid block starts at byte 156, and the DRAM fault model's 8 KB device row
is aligned to the buffer start — so a row anchored anywhere in the centroids covers [0, 8192) and
destroys the entire header on its way. In the E6 sweep that cell crashed 29 of 30 seeds WITH all
three protection layers on, and the crash is the header, not the centroid bytes. Measured
directly: protecting the centroids alone leaves 29 of 30 still crashing; adding the header takes
it to 0 of 30.

FIX. Eight checks between the last field read and the first malloc, each an invariant of what
save() emits, so none of them can reject a legitimate index:

  1/8 padded_dim_ == dim_ rounded up to a multiple of 64 (the FhtKacRotator's requirement);
  2/8 0 < cur_element_count_ <= max_elements_, enterpoint_node_ < cur_element_count_,
      0 <= maxlevel_ <= 64, num_cluster_ > 0;
  3/8 maxM_ == M_ and maxM0_ == 2*M_, the degrees save() derived from M_;
  4/8 size_links_level0_ and size_links_per_element_ follow from those degrees;
  5/8 size_bin_data_ and size_ex_data_ follow from padded_dim_ and ex_bits_ (data_layout.hpp);
  6/8 label_offset_ / offsetBinData_ / offsetExData_ / size_data_per_element_ are the running
      sum of those block sizes;
  7/8 mult_ == 1/log(M_), the level-assignment constant save() derived from M_;
  8/8 the global one: save() writes header, centroids, level0, one [uint32 len][len bytes]
      record per element, then the rotation. Everything but the records has a closed form, so
      the residual IS the record block — and it cannot be smaller than the one length word per
      element save() always emits. Any high-bit flip in a count or size field overshoots here.

COVERAGE, measured not asserted. phase3_e10_header.py enumerates all 156*8 = 1,248 single-bit
header flips against this predicate (artifacts/phase3/e10/header_coverage.json):

    header-only, i.e. what this patch can check   1,129 / 1,248 = 90.46%
    + walking the record stream to EOF            1,172 / 1,248 = 93.91%

The 119 header-only escapes are classified by what they would actually DO, because "undetected"
and "dangerous" are not the same thing:

   19  desync         move a computed offset (num_cluster_ up, cur_element_count_ down), so the
                      sequential read misaligns and every later record plus the rotation come
                      from the wrong place;
   24  level_overrun  send the descent to a level that was never allocated — 4 by raising
                      maxlevel_, and 20 by moving enterpoint_node_ to a DIFFERENT VALID element
                      id. The second is the one that most looks harmless and is not: on this
                      index exactly one element carries a maxlevel*size_links_per_element record
                      (the length histogram is {0: 937001, 68: 59126, 136: 3626, 204: 234,
                      272: 12, 340: 1}), so any other id sends get_linklist past the end of a
                      shorter link list. Replayed against the stock binary: SIGSEGV.
   25  alloc          change only how much room max_elements_ reserves; load and search are
                      byte-identical;
   51  benign         land on a value a legitimate index could hold AND that load() acts on
                      identically, e.g. a lower maxlevel_.

All 43 of the dangerous ones close when the upper-link record stream is walked to EOF, which is
what framing-guard.patch already does. That walk pins three things a header-only check cannot:
the stream must END exactly where the rotation begins, the LONGEST record must equal
maxlevel*size_links_per_element, and the ENTRY POINT's own record must be that longest one. The
two guards are therefore complementary rather than alternatives — this one stops the malloc
before it happens, that one proves the stream the header described is the stream the file
contains. The remaining 76 are values save() itself could have written, and rejecting them would
mean rejecting legitimate indexes, which is the bar framing-guard.patch set for its own bound.

SCOPE. This DETECTS; it does not repair. Repair needs a pristine copy of the header, and on this
path there is none — the file being loaded IS the corrupted one. A caller that still holds the
original index on durable storage can re-read [0, 156) and fix it in place; that is what the
experiment harness does, by putting `header` in the cliff layer's replicated-region list
alongside the rotation and the centroids. The guard supplies the signal, not the recovery policy.

The predicate is stated once, in Python, at qp/rabitq/layout.py (HEADER_CONSTRAINTS), and this
patch mirrors it check for check so the two cannot drift apart unnoticed.

REBUILD-VERIFIED (artifacts/phase3/e10/header_guard_rebuild.json). Built from a scratch copy of
the library; the production bin/ was left untouched so concurrent experiments were unaffected.
Compared against the stock binary on identical bytes, ef=64, k=10:

  |                          | stock                        | guarded    |
  |--------------------------|------------------------------|------------|
  | clean recall@10          | 0.95035                      | 0.95035    |
  | clean top-10 ids         | sha256[:16] fbe27be5c4bd6264 | IDENTICAL  |
  | load+search wall, med/10 | 0.7129 s                     | 0.7111 s   |

The measured difference is -0.0018 s (-0.25%), i.e. the guarded binary came out nominally faster.
That is noise, not a speed-up: the stock binary's own run-to-run spread is 0.0258 s, fourteen
times the difference. The honest statement is that the cost is below the measurement floor, which
is what ~20 integer comparisons executed ONCE per load should look like — unlike the framing
guard's +0.75%, which is 4 comparisons per element across 1,000,000 elements.

Replaying one high-bit flip into each of the 18 load-bearing fields:

  13  rejected by this guard, with a diagnostic naming the field and the value
   1  rejected earlier by existing code (ex_bits_, via select_excode_ipfunc)
   3  crash identically to the stock binary — num_cluster_ (SIGABRT), maxlevel_ (SIGABRT),
      enterpoint_node_ (SIGSEGV): exactly the desync/level_overrun classes the coverage
      analysis predicts a header-only check cannot close
   1  loads and searches normally at recall 0.95035 — max_elements_ raised, a legitimate value

Four control flips outside the header (centroids, level0 payload, upper-link payload, rotation)
all load and search without triggering the guard, so it does not over-trigger. The rotation
control drops recall to 0.82902 while loading cleanly, which is correct: that damage is the
cliff layer's business, not this guard's.

CAVEAT ON THE FAILURE FORM, inherited from framing-guard.patch: the throw is uncaught in
exp_dumpids, so the process ends via std::terminate (exit -6) with the message on stderr. That is
loud and diagnosable, but a caller wanting graceful degradation should wrap load() in try/catch.

Apply from the RaBitQ-Library root, on top of library-changes.patch and recovery-changes.patch:
  git apply /path/to/header-guard.patch

--- a/include/rabitqlib/index/hnsw/hnsw.hpp
+++ b/include/rabitqlib/index/hnsw/hnsw.hpp
@@ -8,11 +8,13 @@
 #include <cassert>
 #include <chrono>  // quant_protection: load-time CRC scan timing (fi_crc_scan_ns_)
 #include <cstddef>
+#include <cmath>
 #include <cstdlib>
 #include <fstream>
 #include <iostream>
 #include <memory>
 #include <mutex>
+#include <string>
 #include <unordered_map>
 #include <vector>
 
@@ -807,6 +809,127 @@
     input.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&mult_), sizeof(double));
     input.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&ef_construction_), sizeof(size_t));
 
+    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // HEADER GUARD. Every field above was read with no validation, and the two mallocs
+    // below size themselves from those fields. A flip in a count or a size field is not a
+    // wrong answer, it is an allocation on garbage: malloc(num_cluster_ * padded_dim_ * 4)
+    // or malloc(max_elements_ * size_data_per_element_) on a value that was never written.
+    // That is why an 8 KB device row anchored in the centroid block crashes rather than
+    // degrades -- the block starts at byte 156, so a buffer-aligned row covers [0, 8192)
+    // and takes all 156 header bytes with it on the way.
+    //
+    // Every check below is an invariant of what save() emits, so none of them can reject a
+    // legitimate index -- the same bar framing-guard.patch set for its length bound. The
+    // predicate is stated once in Python at qp/rabitq/layout.py (HEADER_CONSTRAINTS) and
+    // measured exhaustively over all 1,248 single-bit header flips by
+    // phase3_e10_header.py, so the two cannot drift apart silently.
+    //
+    // NOTE ON SCOPE. This DETECTS; it does not repair. Repair needs a pristine copy of the
+    // header, and on this path there is none: the file being loaded IS the corrupted one.
+    // A caller holding the original index on durable storage can re-read [0, 156) and fix
+    // it in place, which is what the experiment harness does. The guard supplies the
+    // signal, not the recovery policy.
+    {
+        const std::streampos after_header = input.tellg();
+        input.seekg(0, std::ios::end);
+        const std::streamoff file_size = input.tellg();
+        input.seekg(after_header);
+        if (!input) {
+            throw std::runtime_error("Corrupt index: cannot size the file to validate the header");
+        }
+
+        auto reject = [](const std::string& what) {
+            throw std::runtime_error("Corrupt index: implausible header -- " + what);
+        };
+        auto round_up_64 = [](size_t x) { return ((x + 63) / 64) * 64; };
+
+        // 1/8 geometry: padded_dim_ is dim_ rounded up for the FhtKacRotator.
+        if (dim_ == 0 || padded_dim_ != round_up_64(dim_)) {
+            reject("padded_dim " + std::to_string(padded_dim_) + " is not dim " +
+                   std::to_string(dim_) + " rounded up to a multiple of 64");
+        }
+        // 2/8 counts.
+        if (cur_element_count_ == 0 || cur_element_count_ > max_elements_) {
+            reject("cur_element_count " + std::to_string(cur_element_count_) +
+                   " outside (0, max_elements=" + std::to_string(max_elements_) + "]");
+        }
+        if (enterpoint_node_ >= cur_element_count_) {
+            reject("enterpoint_node " + std::to_string(enterpoint_node_) + " >= " +
+                   std::to_string(cur_element_count_));
+        }
+        if (maxlevel_ < 0 || maxlevel_ > 64) {
+            reject("maxlevel " + std::to_string(maxlevel_) + " outside [0, 64]");
+        }
+        if (num_cluster_ == 0) {
+            reject("num_cluster is 0");
+        }
+        // 3/8 degree parameters: save() wrote maxM_ = M_ and maxM0_ = 2*M_.
+        if (M_ == 0 || maxM_ != M_ || maxM0_ != 2 * M_) {
+            reject("degree parameters M=" + std::to_string(M_) + " maxM=" +
+                   std::to_string(maxM_) + " maxM0=" + std::to_string(maxM0_) +
+                   " violate maxM==M and maxM0==2*M");
+        }
+        // 4/8 link-block sizes follow from the degrees (hnsw.hpp save-side arithmetic).
+        if (size_links_level0_ != maxM0_ * sizeof(PID) + sizeof(PID)) {
+            reject("size_links_level0 " + std::to_string(size_links_level0_) +
+                   " != maxM0*sizeof(PID)+sizeof(PID)");
+        }
+        if (size_links_per_element_ != maxM_ * sizeof(PID) + sizeof(PID)) {
+            reject("size_links_per_element " + std::to_string(size_links_per_element_) +
+                   " != maxM*sizeof(PID)+sizeof(PID)");
+        }
+        // 5/8 quantization block sizes follow from padded_dim_ and ex_bits_ (data_layout.hpp).
+        if (ex_bits_ > 16) {
+            reject("ex_bits " + std::to_string(ex_bits_) + " > 16");
+        }
+        const size_t want_bin = padded_dim_ / 8 + 3 * sizeof(float);
+        const size_t want_ex =
+            ex_bits_ > 0 ? padded_dim_ * ex_bits_ / 8 + 2 * sizeof(float) : 0;
+        if (size_bin_data_ != want_bin || size_ex_data_ != want_ex) {
+            reject("size_bin_data/size_ex_data " + std::to_string(size_bin_data_) + "/" +
+                   std::to_string(size_ex_data_) + " != " + std::to_string(want_bin) + "/" +
+                   std::to_string(want_ex) + " for this padded_dim/ex_bits");
+        }
+        // 6/8 element offsets are a running sum of the block sizes above.
+        if (label_offset_ != size_links_level0_ + sizeof(PID) ||
+            offsetBinData_ != size_links_level0_ + 2 * sizeof(PID) ||
+            offsetExData_ != offsetBinData_ + size_bin_data_) {
+            reject("element offsets label/bin/ex " + std::to_string(label_offset_) + "/" +
+                   std::to_string(offsetBinData_) + "/" + std::to_string(offsetExData_) +
+                   " are not the running sum of the block sizes");
+        }
+        if (size_data_per_element_ !=
+            size_links_level0_ + 2 * sizeof(PID) + size_bin_data_ + size_ex_data_) {
+            reject("size_data_per_element " + std::to_string(size_data_per_element_) +
+                   " != links+ids+bin+ex");
+        }
+        // 7/8 mult_ = 1/log(M), the level-assignment constant save() derived from M_.
+        const double want_mult = 1.0 / std::log(static_cast<double>(M_));
+        if (!(std::abs(mult_ - want_mult) <= 1e-9 * std::max(1.0, std::abs(want_mult)))) {
+            reject("mult " + std::to_string(mult_) + " != 1/log(M) = " +
+                   std::to_string(want_mult));
+        }
+        // 8/8 THE GLOBAL ONE. save() writes header, centroids, level0, one
+        // [uint32 len][len bytes] record per element, then the rotation. Everything but the
+        // records has a closed form, so the residual IS the record block -- and it cannot be
+        // smaller than the one length word per element that save() always emits. Any high-bit
+        // flip in a count or size field overshoots the file here.
+        const size_t centroid_bytes = num_cluster_ * padded_dim_ * sizeof(float);
+        const size_t level0_bytes = cur_element_count_ * size_data_per_element_;
+        const size_t rotation_bytes = 4 * padded_dim_ / 8;
+        const size_t fixed = static_cast<size_t>(after_header) + centroid_bytes + level0_bytes +
+                             rotation_bytes;
+        if (static_cast<std::streamoff>(fixed) > file_size ||
+            file_size - static_cast<std::streamoff>(fixed) <
+                static_cast<std::streamoff>(sizeof(unsigned int) * cur_element_count_)) {
+            reject("the blocks account for " + std::to_string(fixed) + " bytes plus at least " +
+                   std::to_string(sizeof(unsigned int) * cur_element_count_) +
+                   " of upper-link length words, but the file is only " +
+                   std::to_string(static_cast<long long>(file_size)) + " bytes");
+        }
+    }
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------ end header guard
+
     centroids_memory_ =
         reinterpret_cast<char*>(malloc(num_cluster_ * padded_dim_ * sizeof(float)));
 
